## Structure (Hexagonal layers)

Each module gets its own slice across all three layers:

```
src/
├── domain/<module>/            ← core: no dependency on NestJS or a database
│   ├── entities/<module>.entity.ts
│   └── repositories/<module>.repository.interface.ts
├── application/<module>/       ← use-cases and DTOs
│   ├── use-cases/
│   └── dto/
└── infrastructure/<module>/    ← adapters: controller, repository implementation
    ├── controllers/
    ├── persistence/
    └── <module>.module.ts
```

Dependency rule: `infrastructure` → `application` → `domain`. Never the other way around.

Each repository implementation is currently in-memory. To connect a real database, write a new implementation of the module's repository interface and swap it in that module's `*.module.ts` — since the use-cases only depend on the interface, nothing else needs to change.

